# Pinned test corpora for the design-review scanners (issue #1075, epic #1067).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. librarian ships no UI code, so the accessibility / i18n /
# UX scanners of #1067 cannot be validated against this repo. They must measure
# precision and recall against real external projects — and a rate is not
# reproducible without a pinned commit. Upstream repos move: a corpus re-cloned
# at HEAD six months from now measures a DIFFERENT codebase, while the earlier
# precision figure sits in docs/verification/ still reading as evidence.
#
# That is the ground-truth-drift version of a shape this repo keeps filing: a
# number that reads as evidence while the thing it measured changed underneath
# it. The pin is what stops it; tests/lint-measurement-citations.sh is what keeps
# the pin honest, by failing closed when a published measurement cites a corpus
# or a SHA this file does not back.
#
# EXPLICIT AND ORDERED, NEVER A GLOB — the same discipline as
# tests/lib/fragments.sh and the workflow.src/ manifests, for the same reason: a
# glob lets an entry drift in unnoticed, and an explicit list is a reviewable
# artifact. Adding a corpus is a deliberate diff that says why.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT — one record per corpus, fields separated by a single TAB:
#
#   name <TAB> url <TAB> sha <TAB> license <TAB> fallback <TAB> why
#
#   name      short slug; the directory under $CORPORA_DIR and the token a
#             measurement cites. [a-z0-9-] only.
#   url       clone URL, https only (no credentials, no ssh — this is fetched on
#             CI-less developer machines).
#   sha       FULL 40-character commit SHA. A short SHA is rejected by the gate:
#             abbreviations are not stable identifiers, and git will happily
#             resolve a 7-char prefix to a different object as a repo grows.
#   license   SPDX identifier, verified against the repo's own metadata. See
#             "License is a real gate" below.
#   fallback  what to do when the pin becomes unreachable (force-push, history
#             rewrite, repo deletion). Either `tag:<name>` or `vendor:<note>`.
#   why       one line: why THIS commit.
#
# Blank lines and #-initial lines are ignored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# LICENSE IS A REAL GATE, NOT A FORMALITY. Nothing here is redistributed — the
# corpora are fetched at measurement time, never vendored into this repo — but
# the license is recorded per entry anyway, and a candidate whose terms make even
# local automated analysis doubtful is replaced rather than rationalized. Every
# entry below is permissive (MIT or MPL-2.0); that is an outcome of applying the
# gate, not a coincidence.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT WAS VERIFIED BEFORE PINNING, AND WHAT DID NOT SURVIVE IT
#
# #1067's candidate list was written by an assistant with a knowledge cutoff and
# says so. Each candidate was checked against the live API before being pinned.
# Three did not survive contact, and the rejections are recorded HERE so the next
# person does not spend the same hour re-deriving them:
#
#   w3c/wai-bad-demo  -- REJECTED, twice over. The "Before and After
#     Demonstration" repo is EMPTY: three files (README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,
#     w3c.json), zero fixtures. It also carries NO license. #1067 called this the
#     "cleanest possible A/B" because it pairs an inaccessible and an accessible
#     version of one site — that pairing is genuinely valuable and is worth
#     re-sourcing from the WAI website repo, but it is NOT at this URL and the
#     slice that wants it should find it rather than inherit a dead pin.
#
#   mastodon/mastodon -- REPLACED as the i18n corpus. AGPL-3.0, and 405 MB.
#     excalidraw/excalidraw is MIT, an eighth the size, and confirmed carrying 59
#     locale files — it fills the "i18n'd project" role with none of the license
#     question. (element-hq/element-web was checked too: AGPL-3.0 and 598 MB.)
#
#   dequelabs/axe-core @ develop -- RE-PINNED. The develop tip moved the same day
#     it was checked, which is precisely the drift this file exists to stop.
#     Pinned at release tag v4.13.0 instead: a tag is a better pin because it is
#     what upstream itself treats as stable, and it gives the fallback something
#     real to name.
#
# DELIBERATELY DEFERRED: shadcn-ui/ui (MIT, 70 MB) as a second near-clean
# negative control. radix-ui/primitives already fills that role at a third the
# size; if #1069's measurement shows one control is not enough, add it there with
# the numbers that justified it.
#
# Fetch cost measured on this recipe (shallow + blobless): axe-core materializes
# at 22 MB, against a GitHub-reported repository size of 23 MB. #1075 measured
# the shallow-vs-full ratio separately on librarian itself — 20 MB against 115 MB
# — so the saving is largest on repos with deep history; these five land around
# 100 MB total either way, not gigabytes. That is what makes a named volume the
# right home rather than something to re-fetch per run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

jsx-a11y	https://github.com/jsx-eslint/eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y.git	8f75961d965e47afb88854d324bd32fafde7acfe	MIT	vendor:__tests__/src/rules fixtures are small and MIT — vendor them if this pin dies	Labeled ground truth: JSX fixtures mapped to expected violations per rule. Main tip at time of pinning.
axe-core	https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core.git	1cc54b900413660610180d631feb73c9e74f4dc9	MPL-2.0	tag:v4.13.0	Labeled ground truth: HTML fixtures with expected results. Release tag v4.13.0, not the develop tip — develop moved the day it was checked.
radix	https://github.com/radix-ui/primitives.git	f7ecd5ab16f5e1e820eb5786a1419a98a2d594ae	MIT	vendor:no vendored fallback — re-pin to a later main commit	Near-clean negative control: accessibility-first primitives. If a scanner lights this up, the scanner is wrong.
excalidraw	https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw.git	a2ec2889babf7d2295469c6d90ebe77fae57df84	MIT	tag:v0.18.1	i18n'd corpus for #1071 (59 locale files). Release tag v0.18.1 — MIT replacement for AGPL Mastodon.
netnewswire	https://github.com/Ranchero-Software/NetNewsWire.git	ae64ffe1792dfd25f943643c2cf90e81476bd06a	MIT	tag:iOS-7.1.3-7206	Apple arm for #1074: mature, accessibility-conscious SwiftUI/UIKit. Release tag iOS-7.1.3-7206.
